<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonard Woolley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1880–1960</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Leonard Woolley, Charles Leonard Woolley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Leonard Woolley was a British archaeologist best known for excavating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1922 to 1934. There he uncovered the Royal Cemetery, with the tomb of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puabi">Puabi</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a> and the golden helmet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a>. He later excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, capital of the Kingdom of Mukish, at Tell Atchana from 1937, recovering palaces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> archives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley.jpg" alt="Leonard Woolley" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hulton-Deutsch Collection · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonard_Woolley_Getty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
